# THE WOOD (THE MAZE), SLAB 4 — 55 rooms. Takes the maze to 170 and CLOSES IT.
#
# rome cut the target from 280 to 170 (2026-08-02). Losability comes from the
# lying cores, not from room count, and the extra 110 would have been mostly
# more honest band — trees you walk past on the way to the part that works.
#
# THE FAR SIDE. This slab's idea: a whole honest quarter of the wood that you
# CANNOT REACH BY WALKING WEST. Its only entrances are `up` climbs out of the
# sunken wood and out of the deeper core below that. You get to the far side by
# going UNDER the maze, which means you get there by being lost, not by
# deciding to. You can always leave it — two rooms lie eastward back into the
# cores, and the cores spill onto the honest band — but you cannot walk back to
# it deliberately.
#
# What's out there: a ruined moated holding, nine rooms of it, the only built
# thing in the whole wood. That is what the far side is FOR.
#
# Also: CORE G, a second sunken layer below the sunken wood, reached by `down`
# from three of its rooms and leaving only by climbing out onto the far side.
# And CORE H, a dry scrub core hanging off the far side's western edge.
#
# Rings of 12 again: north +1, east +3, south +5, west +7.

!region wood

# ---- attachments: the sunken wood grows a ceiling and a floor ---------------
# Three rooms of the sunken wood can be climbed out of, onto the far side.

## the-cold-seep | attachment: a way up
!existing
> up the-long-glade  oneway

## the-white-roots | attachment: a way up
!existing
> up the-standing-water  oneway

## the-bottom-of-it | attachment: a way up
!existing
> up the-far-mire  oneway

# And three of them go down again, into something older.

## the-sunken-wood | attachment: the floor gives too
!existing
> down the-lower-ditch  oneway

## the-buried-wall | attachment: the floor gives too
!existing
> down the-old-course  oneway

## the-green-dark | attachment: the floor gives too
!existing
> down the-still-pool  oneway

# ---- THE FAR SIDE: honest ground, unreachable on foot ----------------------
# Row A (north)

## the-far-birches | The Far Birches
Birch again, the way the wood began on the other side, except that there is no road behind this stand and no gap in it that goes anywhere you have been. The light comes from the wrong direction for the hour.
> west the-wolf-pits
> south the-long-glade
> north the-park-pale
> east the-bracken-sea  oneway

## the-park-pale | The Park Pale
A bank and ditch running north to south with the stumps of a paling fence still in the crest of it, cut and set to keep deer inside a park. There is no park. There has not been a park for a very long time.
> south the-far-birches

## the-wolf-pits | The Wolf Pits
Two deep steep-sided pits with a narrow bar of earth between them, dug where a run comes through, revetted in stone at the lip. Whatever they were dug for was worth this much labour, once.
> east the-far-birches
> west the-north-marches
> south the-keepers-approach
> north the-dovecote

## the-dovecote | The Dovecote
A round stone tower the height of two men, roofless, its whole inner wall stepped with nesting boxes from the floor to the top course. It held several hundred birds and somebody ate them.
> south the-wolf-pits

## the-north-marches | The North Marches
Wide open wood, mature and well spaced, with grass under it instead of leaf-litter — grazed ground, kept that way by something in numbers. It goes on north further than you want to commit to.
> east the-wolf-pits
> west the-edge-of-it
> south the-moat-bank
> north the-fishponds

## the-fishponds | The Fishponds
Three rectangular ponds in a line down the slope, each one feeding the next through a stone sluice, all of them silted to a black flat and grown over with reed. They were stocked and harvested and counted.
> south the-north-marches

## the-edge-of-it | The Edge of It
The wood simply stops. Not a boundary, not a ride, not a change of species — a line, beyond which is grey open country with nothing on it, going away west as far as there is light to see it by.
> east the-north-marches
> south the-west-ride

# Row B

## the-long-glade | The Long Glade
A glade running east to west, narrow and half a mile of it, floored in fine grass. Standing at one end you can see the whole length, and there is nothing in it, and nothing crosses it while you watch.
> north the-far-birches
> west the-keepers-approach
> south the-standing-water

## the-keepers-approach | The Keeper's Approach
An avenue of limes, planted in two lines with forty feet between them, running west. They are grown out of all discipline now but the lines hold. Somebody meant this to be walked up, slowly, toward something.
> east the-long-glade
> west the-moat-bank
> north the-wolf-pits
> south the-orchard-gone-wild

## the-moat-bank | The Moat Bank
The outer bank of a moat, grassed over, with the water still lying in the ditch below it — square-cut, corners intact, going round something. From up here you can see the whole enclosure and what is left standing in it.
> east the-keepers-approach
> west the-west-ride
> north the-north-marches
> south the-yew-walk

## the-west-ride | The West Ride
The last of the rides, running west and ending at a gap in the moat bank where a bridge was. The abutments are there. Everything between them is gone.
> east the-moat-bank
> north the-edge-of-it
> south the-boar-ground
> west the-gate-arch

# Row C

## the-standing-water | The Standing Water
Water lying in a broad shallow sheet over the wood floor with no bank to it, ankle-deep and still, the trees going down into their own reflections. It has no source and no outlet and it does not drain.
> north the-long-glade
> west the-orchard-gone-wild
> south the-far-mire

## the-orchard-gone-wild | The Orchard Gone Wild
Fruit trees in rows, unpruned for a century, grown tall and thin and reaching, still bearing something small and hard. Between the rows the grass is deep. Somebody's apples, still coming, for nobody.
> east the-standing-water
> west the-yew-walk
> north the-keepers-approach
> south the-sunken-lawn

## the-yew-walk | The Yew Walk
Yews planted in a double row, enormous now, their crowns grown into one another to make a tunnel that keeps out weather and light in equal measure. The floor of it is bare red needle and nothing else.
> east the-orchard-gone-wild
> west the-boar-ground
> north the-moat-bank
> south the-broken-avenue

## the-boar-ground | The Boar Ground
Ground rooted up wholesale — turf turned in sheets, saplings pushed over, the whole stretch worked at by something heavy and unhurried. It is not old and it is not finished.
> east the-yew-walk
> north the-west-ride
> south the-holly-hedge
> west the-thorn-waste  oneway

# Row D (south)

## the-far-mire | The Far Mire
Wet ground again at the wood's far southern corner, pale and quaking, with the trees standing in it thinly and leaning. Something has crossed it in a straight line and the line is still holding its shape.
> north the-standing-water
> west the-sunken-lawn
> south the-stew-pond
> east the-willow-break  oneway

## the-stew-pond | The Stew Pond
A pond dug and lined and provided with a stone sill, for keeping fish alive until they were wanted. It has held its shape perfectly and it is entirely full of black leaf-mud.
> north the-far-mire

## the-sunken-lawn | The Sunken Lawn
A rectangle of ground a foot lower than everything around it, level as a table, its edges too straight to be anything but cut. Grass, close and fine, and nothing growing on it taller than your ankle.
> east the-far-mire
> west the-broken-avenue
> north the-orchard-gone-wild

## the-broken-avenue | The Broken Avenue
Another planted line, this one of beech, and half of it down — every third or fourth tree fallen in the same direction on the same night, and the survivors standing on in the gaps.
> east the-sunken-lawn
> west the-holly-hedge
> north the-yew-walk
> south the-hollow-yew

## the-hollow-yew | The Hollow Yew
One yew, alone, older than the planted ones by centuries, hollow enough to stand inside with room to turn round. The inside is dry and dark and smells of nothing at all.
> north the-broken-avenue

## the-holly-hedge | The Holly Hedge
A hedge of holly laid and woven by hand, grown out now to twice its intended height but still visibly a made thing, running north to south. It marks the last enclosed edge of the holding.
> east the-broken-avenue
> north the-boar-ground
> south the-ash-heap

## the-ash-heap | The Ash Heap
A mound of white wood-ash and clinker, dumped over years, grown over with nettle at the edges and still bare on top. Whatever burned here burned steadily and for a long time.
> north the-holly-hedge

# ---- THE HOLDING: the only built thing in the wood --------------------------

## the-gate-arch | The Gate Arch
A stone arch standing on its own, the gatehouse it belonged to gone from around it, with the sockets for the gate-pins still cut in the jambs. The road under it is still paved for twenty feet in each direction.
> east the-west-ride
> south the-well-court
> north the-stable-range

## the-stable-range | The Stable Range
A long low range with the mangers still in it, twelve bays of them, and the tethering rings still set in the wall. The roof went first and everything else has been going since.
> south the-gate-arch

## the-well-court | The Well Court
A courtyard with a wellhead in the middle of it, the winding gear gone but the shaft open and lined and dropping further than your eye follows. Grass has come up between every flag.
> north the-gate-arch
> east the-kitchen-range
> west the-chapel-shell
> south the-hall-floor

## the-kitchen-range | The Kitchen Range
Two enormous hearths back to back in a wall that is otherwise mostly gone, the flues still standing up out of it, the stone burned to a soft white for six feet above each opening.
> west the-well-court
> south the-fish-house

## the-fish-house | The Fish House
A small vaulted building set into the ground with stone tanks in the floor, three of them, fed once from the moat. They hold rainwater now, and something is living in them.
> north the-kitchen-range

## the-chapel-shell | The Chapel Shell
Four walls and no roof, the window openings still traced in cut stone, and a floor of grave-slabs worn smooth and unreadable. Whatever this family was, they were buried in their own house.
> east the-well-court

## the-hall-floor | The Hall Floor
The great hall, reduced to its floor and the stumps of its walls: a rectangle of paving with a hearth in the middle of it, open to the sky, big enough that the roof over it must have been a considerable idea.
> north the-well-court
> south the-solar
> east the-icehouse

## the-solar | The Solar
The private end, one room's worth, up three steps from the hall floor and better built than the rest — dressed ashlar, a fireplace with carving on the lintel, a window that faced the garden.
> north the-hall-floor

## the-icehouse | The Icehouse
A brick dome half buried in the bank with a low passage into it, and the passage is clear. Inside, the air is markedly colder than outside, which after this long is not something the brick is doing.
> west the-hall-floor

# ---- CORE G: below the sunken wood. The deepest ground in the wood ----------
# Ring of 12. Entered by three falls from the sunken wood, left only by two
# climbs onto the far side. From the bottom, the way out is further up than
# you came down.

## the-lower-ditch | The Lower Ditch
A cut running through the dark at the bottom of everything, revetted with rough stone, going straight in both directions. It was open to the sky once. It has a wood's worth of ground over it now.
> north the-flint-floor  oneway
> east the-drip-line  oneway
> south the-low-sump  oneway
> west the-clay-shelf  oneway

## the-flint-floor | The Flint Floor
The floor here is a pavement of knapped flint set on edge, close-packed, laid by hand over a wide area and buried since. It rings faintly underfoot, which nothing else down here does.
> north the-black-loam  oneway
> east the-old-course  oneway
> south the-tree-fall  oneway
> west the-still-pool  oneway

## the-black-loam | The Black Loam
Soil so dark and fine it takes a print like wax and gives nothing back, deep enough that the tree roots reaching down into it never find a bottom. It smells rich and wrong.
> north the-drip-line  oneway
> east the-low-sump  oneway
> south the-clay-shelf  oneway
> west the-buried-lane  oneway

## the-drip-line | The Drip Line
Water comes down from the ceiling of roots in a hundred separate threads, steadily, into pools it has worn into the floor over an unhurried span of time. The sound is continuous and covers everything.
> north the-old-course  oneway
> east the-tree-fall  oneway
> south the-still-pool  oneway
> west the-last-light  oneway

## the-old-course | The Old Course
A riverbed, dry, with its gravel graded and its banks intact, running through the dark under the wood. The river left. Everything it built is exactly where it was.
> north the-low-sump  oneway
> east the-clay-shelf  oneway
> south the-buried-lane  oneway
> west the-under-roots  oneway
> up the-yew-walk  oneway

## the-low-sump | The Low Sump
The low point, where everything that comes down here collects: black water without a current, wide, and deeper at the middle than you would test. Nothing moves on it.
> north the-tree-fall  oneway
> east the-still-pool  oneway
> south the-last-light  oneway
> west the-lower-ditch  oneway

## the-tree-fall | The Tree Fall
A tree came down through the ground from above and is still here, most of it, jammed at an angle from the floor to the roots overhead, gone soft and pale and mounded with fungus.
> north the-clay-shelf  oneway
> east the-buried-lane  oneway
> south the-under-roots  oneway
> west the-flint-floor  oneway

## the-clay-shelf | The Clay Shelf
A ledge of grey clay running out across the dark, smooth, wet, and holding every mark ever made on it. There are a great many marks and they are of several different kinds.
> north the-still-pool  oneway
> east the-last-light  oneway
> south the-lower-ditch  oneway
> west the-black-loam  oneway

## the-still-pool | The Still Pool
Water with a surface like a laid floor, absolutely unmoving, reflecting the roots above it so exactly that it takes a moment to work out which way up you are looking.
> north the-buried-lane  oneway
> east the-under-roots  oneway
> south the-flint-floor  oneway
> west the-drip-line  oneway

## the-buried-lane | The Buried Lane
A sunken track with banks on both sides and a hard rutted floor, running through the underground dark exactly as it would run through open country. It was a lane. The ground came up over it.
> north the-last-light  oneway
> east the-lower-ditch  oneway
> south the-black-loam  oneway
> west the-old-course  oneway

## the-last-light | The Last Light
A shaft of grey comes down from somewhere far overhead through a hole in the root-ceiling, and lands here, and lights a circle of floor about the size of a table. It is the only daylight down here.
> north the-under-roots  oneway
> east the-flint-floor  oneway
> south the-drip-line  oneway
> west the-low-sump  oneway
> up the-broken-avenue  oneway

## the-under-roots | The Under-Roots
The ceiling is entirely roots, woven together into a mat you could not put a knife through, and they come down to the floor in places in columns. It is holding up the whole wood, and you are underneath it.
> north the-lower-ditch  oneway
> east the-black-loam  oneway
> south the-old-course  oneway
> west the-tree-fall  oneway

# ---- CORE H: the dry scrub core, off the far side's western edge ------------
# Ring of 12. Two exits swapped for spills back onto the far side.

## the-thorn-waste | The Thorn Waste
Thorn scrub over broken ground, head-high, in no order, with narrow ways through it that were made by something lower than you. It goes on in every direction at about the same height.
> north the-grey-scrub  oneway
> east the-gorse-brake  oneway
> south the-heath-edge  oneway
> west the-rabbit-warren  oneway

## the-grey-scrub | The Grey Scrub
Everything here is the same grey-green and the same height and none of it is a tree. The wood has given out and not been replaced by anything with a shape.
> north the-broken-ground  oneway
> east the-sand-cut  oneway
> south the-stunted-oaks  oneway
> west the-dry-heath  oneway

## the-broken-ground | The Broken Ground
Ground worked over and abandoned: shallow pits and heaps in no pattern, all of them grown over, all of them the same age. Somebody dug for something here across a wide area and either found it or gave up.
> north the-gorse-brake  oneway
> east the-heath-edge  oneway
> south the-rabbit-warren  oneway
> west the-pale-grass  oneway

## the-gorse-brake | The Gorse Brake
Gorse in a solid mass, flowering, armed on every inch, with dry dead growth underneath it a foot deep. The smell is coconut and the noise underfoot is like walking on kindling.
> north the-sand-cut  oneway
> east the-stunted-oaks  oneway
> south the-dry-heath  oneway
> west the-flint-scatter  oneway

## the-sand-cut | The Sand Cut
A face of clean yellow sand standing eight feet high where something cut into the rise, with martin holes drilled along the top of it in a row and the spoil fanned out below.
> north the-heath-edge  oneway
> east the-rabbit-warren  oneway
> south the-pale-grass  oneway
> west the-wind-gap  oneway

## the-heath-edge | The Heath Edge
Heather and bare sand, low and open, with the last of the scrub behind you and nothing in front but more of the same going away to a horizon that is much closer than it should be.
> north the-stunted-oaks  oneway
> east the-dry-heath  oneway
> south the-flint-scatter  oneway
> west the-thorn-waste  oneway

## the-stunted-oaks | The Stunted Oaks
Oaks that never got anywhere — forty of them, none taller than a man, all of them a century old at least, gnarled and thick-trunked and knee-high in heather.
> north the-rabbit-warren  oneway
> east the-boar-ground  oneway
> south the-wind-gap  oneway
> west the-grey-scrub  oneway

## the-rabbit-warren | The Rabbit Warren
Sand worked through and through with burrows until the whole rise is hollow, the ground giving slightly wherever you put your weight. The cropped turf between the holes is very short and very green.
> north the-pale-grass  oneway
> east the-flint-scatter  oneway
> south the-thorn-waste  oneway
> west the-broken-ground  oneway

## the-dry-heath | The Dry Heath
Open heath, dry to the root, with a wind moving over it that has nothing to catch on. After the wood the sky is an unreasonable amount of sky.
> north the-flint-scatter  oneway
> east the-wind-gap  oneway
> south the-grey-scrub  oneway
> west the-sand-cut  oneway

## the-pale-grass | The Pale Grass
Grass bleached almost white, growing in tufts out of sand, with the bare ground showing between every tuft. Nothing is grazing it and nothing has trodden it.
> north the-wind-gap  oneway
> east the-thorn-waste  oneway
> south the-broken-ground  oneway
> west the-heath-edge  oneway

## the-flint-scatter | The Flint Scatter
The surface is more flint than soil, broken nodules everywhere, and a good number of them are worked — struck flakes, cores, an edge here and there, lying where they were dropped.
> north the-holly-hedge  oneway
> east the-grey-scrub  oneway
> south the-sand-cut  oneway
> west the-stunted-oaks  oneway

## the-wind-gap | The Wind-Gap
A saddle in the rise where the wind comes through in a steady stream and has scoured the ground to bare sand and stone. It is the highest point out here and it shows you nothing you recognise.
> north the-thorn-waste  oneway
> east the-broken-ground  oneway
> south the-heath-edge  oneway
> west the-pale-grass  oneway
